WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:07.050
Good day, Jai Shri Mataji! I would like to share a little about the

2
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:13.620
tool we have for making the work on

3
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.620
translations easier.

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.240
On the screen is a short plan we will try to go through, and maybe split it into several

5
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.160
videos. First of all: what the motivation was.

6
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:30.620
Of course, to make – as each of us can here – pure knowledge more

7
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.900
accessible to Ukrainian yogis and newcomers –

8
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.860
that part is clear. Taking the mechanical work off the translators –

9
00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:48.230
this is about the point where we have an automatic translation and it

10
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.790
makes the same mistakes in every talk,

11
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.550
not understanding that it is Shri Mataji speaking,

12
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:04.530
or simply the same corrections that we may have across all the talks.

13
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:11.950
So the translator's attention goes to fixing all these things instead of

14
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.850
giving that attention to the truly subtler points

15
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:25.090
as a Sahaja Yogi translator. The mechanical work of whoever makes the subtitles

16
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.590
is quite a lot of work.

17
00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:35.490
Especially if we talk about the fourth point: subtitles of proper quality.

18
00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:42.260
To make subtitles of proper quality, comfortable to read,

19
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:48.440
depending on the talk, it is sometimes not that easy and takes a lot of

20
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:54.780
time. Especially with fast speech, making it comfortable to read

21
00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.780
is a real challenge.

22
00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.340
And so, if we hand this task over to algorithms, the algorithms can

23
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.350
cope with it better.

24
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.630
Those, in fact, were the motivations.

25
00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.750
And that is how this project was born.

26
00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:20.740
Its code is written entirely not by hand but, in fact, also by

27
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.580
artificial intelligence agents,

28
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.850
which lets us develop this tool and keep it

29
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:35.530
at a proper level without spending too much time on it.

30
00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.990
I should say right away what limitations this tool has. It is designed

31
00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:46.450
to work with materials from Amruta. Although it has no direct integration,

32
00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.510
the idea is that it works first of all with materials from Amruta.

33
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.290
Further on we will look at the different components, and there are ways to use this

34
00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:03.030
project for other materials, but not to the full extent. There is also

35
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:09.230
a limitation right now: our auto-translation system runs on a paid, private

36
00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.490
subscription to the Claude Code agent.

37
00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:21.170
So right now it is my personal one, and I also use it for my

38
00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:27.370
main job. That is why I watch the limits. For example, today, if we look,

39
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.510
there is still a lot of time until 9 pm,

40
00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.070
and a lot of the limits are still unused.

41
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.570
There is a weekly limit and a five-hour limit.

42
00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:45.690
So for now, launching it at any moment is mostly always

43
00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:51.610
possible, but depending on the volume, that is, if a lot of material is needed at once,

44
00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:57.410
I check these limits before launching, and fit the launch time

45
00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:01.010
around my own work.

46
00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:07.910
That is the limitation for now, but if someone has another subscription of their own, or, again,

47
00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:12.870
to use parts of the system, that is possible even with

48
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:18.200
free AI tools.

49
00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:26.150
That is it about limitations. So for now anyone can add materials, but

50
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:32.930
launching this active translation phase currently goes through me,

51
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.920
because it is a paid subscription.

52
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.420
If someone also has their own paid subscription, we can add it to the list

53
00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:46.810
of supported ones, and other participants will then be able to

54
00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.330
launch on their own subscriptions, watching their own limits.

55
00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:56.910
If such cases come up, we will build that feature, and it will be possible to

56
00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:03.730
work that way too. But take, for example, just recently, last week there was

57
00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:10.730
a case where many dozens of talks had to be translated without

58
00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:16.240
making subtitles, for work on a book.

59
00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:22.460
And within a week those dozens fit into the limits,

60
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.300
so it is not the case

61
00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.270
that this has to be a blocker.

62
00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:35.070
Most often you can launch translations right away, and if not at that moment, then

63
00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.070
in the evening.
